torname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186221989 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186221990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186221990 1071 "Consider the system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n> $L3$: $det(Φ(t)) \\ne 0$ for all $t$\n> $L4$: $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from line $L2$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices." 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186221990 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices." 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186221990 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the properties of determinants}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices." 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186222503 1064 "Again, let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The fundamental matrices have the following important properties.\n\n- *Satisfies the underlying system*. Indeed, since each column of $\\Phi(t)$ is a solution of the system, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi'(t) = A\\,\\Phi(t).\n$$\n\n- *Invertibility*. The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t,$ so\n\n$$\n\\det\\Phi(t)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t.$ In particular, $\\Phi(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$\n\n- *Representation of solutions*. Every solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\n- *Non-uniqueness*. If $C$ is any invertible constant matrix, then\n\n$$\n\\Psi(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,C\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental matrix for the same system.\n\nWe'll now get some practice." 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186222503 1064 "Again, let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The fundamental matrices have the following important properties.\n\n1\\. *Satisfies the underlying system*. Indeed, since each column of $\\Phi(t)$ is a solution of the system, we have\n\n  $$\n  \\Phi'(t) = A\\,\\Phi(t).\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Invertibility*. The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t,$ so\n\n  $$\n  \\det\\Phi(t)\\neq 0\n  $$\n\n  for all $t.$ In particular, $\\Phi(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$\n\n3\\. *Representation of solutions*. Every solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system can be written in the form\n\n  $$\n  \\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,\\mathbf{c},\n  $$\n\n  where $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\n4\\. *Non-uniqueness*. If $C$ is any invertible constant matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  \\Psi(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,C\n  $$\n\n  is also a fundamental matrix for the same system.\n\nWe'll now get some practice." 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186307700 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442492 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186307700 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305212 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186307700 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442492 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186307701 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442493 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186307701 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305540 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186307701 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442493 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186307871 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442528 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186307871 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307378 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186307871 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442528 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186308289 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442511 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186308289 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306553 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186308289 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442511 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186310795 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309929 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186310795 1078 #edb/ref 17592186308757 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186310795 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309929 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186310826 1075 #edb/ref 17592186308856 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186310826 1078 #edb/ref 17592186308856 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186310826 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309969 13194139534883 17592186045417 true] [17592186311288 1075 #edb/ref 17592186308837 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [17592186311288 1078 #edb/ref 17592186308837 13194139534883 17592186045417 false] [175921